## Why This Is Asked
Tests understanding of 128-bit bit packing, non-overlapping fields, and in-place updates without loops. It exercises careful mask calculation and cross-boundary writes.

## Key Concepts
- Bit masks and clearing specific ranges
- Cross-boundary placement across lo/hi
- Safe in-place updates without branching
- Endianness-agnostic bit numbering

## Code Example
```c
void packHeader128(uint64_t hi, uint64_t lo, uint8_t A, uint16_t B, uint16_t C, uint32_t D, uint64_t *out_hi, uint64_t *out_lo) {
  uint64_t lo_mask = ((uint64_t)0xFF) | (((uint64_t)0xFFF) << 8) | (((uint64_t)0xFFFF) << 32);
  uint64_t hi_mask = ((uint64_t)0xFFFFFFFF) << 16;
  *out_lo = (lo & ~lo_mask) | (uint64_t)A | (((uint64_t)B) << 8) | (((uint64_t)C) << 32);
  *out_hi = (hi & ~hi_mask) | (((uint64_t)D) << 16);
}
``` 

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you adapt if ranges could overlap and you needed deterministic behavior?
- How would you validate correctness across all inputs in a unit test?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to derive bit-run counts without loops, handling 128-bit arithmetic and portable popcounts.

## Key Concepts

- Detect run starts with mask m = x & ~(x << 1).
- Implement 128-bit left shift across hi/lo: (hi,lo) << 1 becomes (hi<<1 | lo>>63, lo<<1).
- Compute popcount on a 128-bit value by summing 64-bit SWAR popcounts on hi and lo.

## Code Example

```javascript
// C-like pseudocode
typedef struct { uint64_t hi; uint64_t lo; } uint128;
static inline uint128 shl1(uint128 x){
  uint128 r; r.hi = (x.hi << 1) | (x.lo >> 63); r.lo = x.lo << 1; return r; }
static inline unsigned popcount64(uint64_t x){
  x = x - ((x >> 1) & 0x5555555555555555ULL);
  x = (x & 0x3333333333333333ULL) + ((x >> 2) & 0x3333333333333333ULL);
  x = (x + (x >> 4)) & 0x0f0f0f0f0f0f0f0fULL;
  return (x * 0x0101010101010101ULL) >> 56; }
unsigned countRuns128(uint128 x){
  uint128 t = shl1(x);
  uint128 m; m.hi = x.hi & ~t.hi; m.lo = x.lo & ~t.lo;
  return popcount64(m.hi) + popcount64(m.lo);
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you leverage hardware intrinsics for popcount on supported platforms?
- How would you scale the approach to 256-bit or 512-bit widths?

## Why This Is Asked

